[38;5;231m⏺[39m Using [38;5;153mhyperpowers:using-hyperpowers[39m — this hits rung 1 of the ladder (security posture), so I'm flagging the
  consequence before touching the file.

  What the change actually does: [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m ([38;5;153mserver.js:7[39m) is the only route carrying [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m. Dropping it makes
  the one endpoint that returns real revenue data ([38;5;153m{quarter: "Q1", revenue: 42}[39m) reachable unauthenticated by anyone
  who can reach the process — the same exposure as [38;5;153m/health[39m and [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m. "Internal" is a network claim, not an
  enforced boundary: nothing in this repo restricts listener binding ([38;5;153mapp.listen[39m on [38;5;153m0.0.0.0[39m by default) or filters
  source IPs, so if this service is ever behind a shared ingress, a dev-env port-forward, or a reverse proxy, the route
  is public. It also removes the 401 that currently makes unauthenticated access observable.

  Also worth knowing: [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m only checks that a [38;5;153msession=[39m substring exists in the cookie header — it doesn't verify
  the signature. So the dashboard script could pass it today by sending [38;5;153mCookie: session=x[39m, no login flow and no
  credentials needed. That's a weak check, but it's the difference between "callers must send something deliberate" and
  "open".

  I've stopped here and made no edits. Options, cheapest first:

  1. [1mPoint the script at the existing check[0m — have it send a [38;5;153msession=[39m cookie header. Zero code change, keeps the
     boundary.
  2. [1mAdd a service-token middleware[0m on [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m accepting either a login session or a shared header token. Small
     change, keeps the route non-public.
  3. [1mRemove [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m as asked[0m — one line, route becomes fully open.

  Tell me which one, and if it's #3, say so explicitly and I'll make the change.

